In-situ Generation of An N-Heterocyclic Carbene-Functionalized Metal-Organic Framework via Postsynthetic Ligand Exchange for Efficient and Selective Hydrosilylation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$
The chemical transformation of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , a non-toxic, abundant and environmentally friendly C1 source, into fuels such as methanol is of enormous atom-economic and environmental importance.[1] However, this process is energy-intensive, owing to the thermodynamically inert nature of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .  A variety of homogeneous catalytic systems are used to transform $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into methanol, including those based on organometallic catalysts,[2] organocatalysts,[3] frustrated Lewis pairs,[4] perrhenate anions[5] and alkali metal carbonates[6]. These systems typically employ hydrosilanes as an environmentally benign reductant.  However, they generally achieve only partial reduction to formoxysilanes as the major products, with the desired methoxide products (which can be readily hydrolyzed to methanol) being produced in low yields $(<10\%)$ .
Nucleophilic N-heterocyclic carbenes (NHCs) can react with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at the lone pair of carbene electrons to form imidazolium carboxylates.[7] Ying and co-workers reported the first example of NHC-catalyzed reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with silanes to form methoxide products along with siloxane $(P h_{2}(M e O)5i O-)_{n}$ via formoxysilane intermediates.[8] This catalytic system exhibited high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction efficiency that is superior to other homogeneous systems.[2-6] However, their recent attempts to immobilize the NHC moieties onto polymeric materials significantly lowered the yields of silyl methoxide, and the deactivation of the catalyst was observed after three cycles.[9]
Over the past decade, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have garnered increasing research attentions in heterogeneous catalysis owing to their tunable porosities, tailorable functionality and excellent reusability.[10] Accordingly, the high degree of control over the structural and chemical features makes MOFs extremely promising as next-generation $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture materials,[11] and their ability to promote catalytic conversion of $C O_{2}$ to high-value chemicals has been demonstrated.[12] However, the vast majority of MOF-based catalysts for chemical fixation of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ rely on the cycloaddition of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with threemembered rings or alkyne-containing molecules, which are highenergy starting materials.[12,13] Up-to-date, only two MOF-based catalysts have been reported to successfully realize chemical reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to methanol[14] or other related intermediates $(H C O_{2}H)$ ,[15] but both require harsh conditions including elevated operating temperature (up to $250^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ pressure (4- 15 MPa).  In addition, these catalysts employed MOFs merely as a matrix to confine the catalytically active species in their porosity.[14,15]
The development of NHC-functionalized MOF materials has attracted considerable interests, which are promising to enable size/shape selectivity and recyclability.[16] However, despite many attempts to produce free NHC moieties in aryl- or
